# rm283, the graduated doorstep (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm283
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier full
written_for rm_hester_fogstack
written_by codex
title the graduated doorstep
fits stage=adult|old
prefers role=merchant talk=plain
traits temper=even talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=content
quirk sets_marked_clay_feet_under_stores_to_read_floodwater
likes mutton_stew swimming festival_bells frosty_mornings
dislikes salt_wind stale_bread
fears a_high_shelf_with_a_soft_foot
hopes a_dry_reserve_at_every_tide
topics work +0.6 weather +0.5 prices +0.4 home +0.2
styles comfort=practical gossip=measured refuse=level tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# sets graduated clay feet under provisions, measuring a flood by the last dry notch and a good day by what remains sound.

words
oath! = Four drowned notches | By the bottom groove
saying! = Read the dry side first | A store stands on its feet
foot = the little clay foot | my grooved block
notch = the second groove | a shallow mark
shelf = the reserve shelf | a high plank
cellar = a low store | the lower room
sack = a sacking bundle | a bound sack
grain = the dry meal | barley grain
saltjar = a covered salt jar | the salt crock
twine = the tag string | a knotted thread
tally = the stores list | my dry tally
stock = a little reserve | goods held back
stew = a ladle of mutton stew | the rich end of the pot
shore = a gravel shelf | a shallow landing
rag = an old rag | a dry cloth
brick = a fired block | hard red clay
mark = a scored ring | one level groove
hinge = the lid strap | the iron fastening
dawnsky = a whitening patch | a pale opening
ripple = a narrow ripple | a curl of water

any
opinion @1 merchants mixed 0 : I judge a seller by the condition of the bottom sack. Fine grain on top proves little.
opinion @1 houses against 1 : Water reaches the store door long before a request for repairs reaches a House's upper rooms.
opinion @1 dole for 2 : A reserve has a purpose. Feeding a hungry wolf fulfils it.
opinion @1 sump for 1 : The lower streets know the worth of three dry boards better than a surveyor on a roof.
opinion @1 watch against 1 : A wet cellar wants carrying paws. A recital of the rules won't raise the sacks.
opinion @1 road for 1 : A road that stays dry is worth a bend or two round a hill.
opinion @1 festivals for 1 : The first bell of a feast makes even a quiet counter feel part of the town.
opinion @1 newcomers for 1 : Somebody seeing these streets afresh may notice a dip we've all grown used to stepping over.
opinion @1 times against 1 : If it costs more to fill a crock each month the glaze doesn't hide how low the level gets.
opinion @1 city for 1 : Our city enough resembles a staircase that a dry board can generally be found somewhere above the wet one.

grown
line @1 greet : Here we have reasonably level footing. Welcome. | Hello. I've just found a place to set the load.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : I don't believe we've shared a dry step before. | Welcome. We can make a beginning on plain ground.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. Clear light for taking stock. | The day is open. My count can wait a moment.
line @1 greet.return : Hello to you in turn. I'm ready now. | Heard that plainly over the water.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : A familiar face is welcome on this shelf of ground. | You're company I can leave a tally open beside.
line @3 greet.return [here.time=night] : Hello after dark. I'll shift this lamp nearer. | Night has brought us within speaking distance.
line @1 again.open : We've both come back to the same mark. | Another meeting above the waterline.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Take a moment and look at this dry mark. | Here, near the little stack of red blocks.
line @1 farewell : May the road keep its crown above water. | Leave with a dry load and enough slack in the ties.
line @1 farewell.return : Goodbye. I'll keep one place clear here. | Step carefully past the low ground.
line @1 react.news : Give me whatever facts are definite. | I'll put off counting till you've told me.
line @1 react.news.new : Nothing about that had reached my side of the counter. | I'll give that a fresh place in my reckoning.
line @1 react.news.known : I have that set down in my memory already. | There's the account I heard earlier.
line @1 react.news.doubt : I'd check against another mark before accepting the level. | Something seems to have shifted in the telling.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|crime|assault|loss|theft] : You've come ashore bowed under that. Let it rest here awhile. | I can stand with you while the weight of it settles.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|work] : Fine news. I'd put a little good food aside to mark it. | That's a sound place for the day to rest.
line @1 ask.how : How has your reserve of strength held up? | Was there any clear space left in your day?
line @1 answer.how : Steady enough to read a fine mark. | I've had a busy stretch and a solid meal.
line @1 decline : I'd be promising something already set aside for another purpose. | It would put my previous promise in doubt.
line @1 accept_decline : I'll record the refusal and let it rest. | That's a clear answer. My count can stand.
line @1 preclose : I'm coming to the last dry minute I can spare. | My rounds need finishing in an orderly fashion.
line @1 accept_preclose : Your account with this conversation is quite square. | I can finish the count on my own now.
line @1 thank : You've saved good things from going to waste. Thanks. | I appreciate the trouble it took to keep that sound.
line @1 deflect_thanks : I had a little in reserve to give. | It was a small enough addition to the round.
line @1 sympathise : Rest your load above the damp with mine. | There's no call to add hurry to what's already heavy.
line @1 reassure : We know how high it's come. That's a beginning. | Let's put the most necessary thing somewhere safe first.
line @1 ack : I'm following the mark you indicate. | That's clear upon a level reading.
line @1 give.advice : Check the foot of the stack before adding height. | A jar lasts longer with air underneath.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : There's a high dry spot set aside for family. | Kin can share the better end of the board.
line @4 greet.return [self.working=yes] : Welcome. I'll put the count aside for a word. | I've reached a convenient pause in the tally.
line @2 farewell.return [here.time=night] : May your lamp show up every low patch. | A dry step home through the dark.
line @1 ask.detail : Which level had the water reached? | What part of it would bear a closer look?
line @1 answer.detail : It left a mark just where the light catches the clay. | There was a dark ring under the lowest notch.
line @1 assess : Fine to leave on the good shelf. | That measure ought to bear another inspection.
line @1 react.self : I can picture the weight of that. | That describes an awkward thing to store.
line @1 tell.plan : I mean to save a dry board for the last bundle. | I'll take a second reading once it's settled.
line @1 give.opinion : A good reserve ought to be readily reached. | The clearest mark is worth the extra minute.
scene footmark
when topic=work self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I set a grooved clay foot under the lowest sack. | There's a water gauge bearing the corner of that shelf.
b : How can a foot be a gauge? | What does the clay tell you?
a : Where water stopped. The bottom groove stays dark after the floor looks dry. | How damp the room got while I was looking at something else.

scene reserve
when topic=work self.role=merchant self.working=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Front sacks mustn't hide the reserve. | I leave my least convenient shelf partly empty.
b : Why waste selling space? | Is the rest due in later?
a : Space to shift things before water finds them pays for itself. | It's room to move goods I already have. That's its job.

scene sleep_stock
when topic=work self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Closing the shutters doesn't stop the stores needing air. | A closed room will quietly spoil a decent sack.
b : Even when the floor is dry? | How do you air the back of a row?
a : Yes. Moisture comes out of the grain as well as up through the boards. | A finger of space is enough to start with. More if the room runs close.

scene swell_wood
when topic=work self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I leave room beside a bin lid for the wood to swell. | A tight lid can become an extraordinary lid when wet.
b : Extraordinarily waterproof? | Would you leave a gap?
a : Extraordinarily hard to open just when the contents need airing. | Only outside the lip. The goods are still covered.

scene poor_counter
when topic=prices self.wealth=poor
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : There's a difference between possessing goods and having money to spend. | Full shelves can belong to a thin purse.
b : But the goods have value. | What keeps you short?
a : Yes, and replacing goods sold has a cost. The gap is my living. | Paying for the next supply before spending the proceeds of the last.

scene tally_sale
when topic=prices self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A small order gets its mark at the moment I set it down. | I keep two counts when there's damp about.
b : Why write down a little order? | Two counts of the same goods?
a : The little ones are the easiest to lose sight of on a full counter. | Dry and suspect. I price only what I can stand behind.

scene show_cut
when topic=prices
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : If a sack is suspect I want the place opened for a look. | Show me where the damp got in before we talk price.
b : Doesn't the smell give it away? | You'd spoil a sound sack?
a : Sometimes the surface dries first. My nose can't look round corners. | A tied mouth opens without a cut. Sound goods will come through the inspection.

scene flood_rise
when topic=weather here.weather=rain
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : This rain will find the low door before the roof has stopped dripping. | A first puddle near the threshold is handier than a reading of the sky.
b : Where's the next place you check? | Does one puddle mean a flood?
a : Whether the little gutter beside it is still carrying water away. | No. If the gutter drains faster than the drops fall, the feet stay dry.

scene wet_after
when topic=weather here.weather=clear
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The shelves want checking as soon as the sky clears. | Dry weather's a good time to read old tide marks.
b : Why not at the height of the shower? | What's left to read?
a : During the shower they all look equally wet. After it the dark remains low down. | The last damp groove tells you how much trouble the next shower might cause.

scene wind_lid
when topic=weather self.dislikes=salt_wind
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Salt wind puts a white edge on my jar lids. | My paws can feel a shore breeze the morning after it blows.
b : How would a lid tell you? | Are your paws sore?
a : A scrape of the edge leaves salt on a dark cloth. Fresh water takes it off. | Not once washed. I take better care of my skin than a tin lid can.

scene cold_notch
when topic=season self.likes=frosty_mornings
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : A little frost picks out every line on a clay foot. | I like the clean white rings a cold morning leaves.
b : Does it spoil the reading? | Even the shallow grooves?
a : For a wet reading, yes. For spotting a chipped ring it's excellent. | First of all. A small scrape leaves a long thin white thread.

scene stewline
when topic=food self.likes=mutton_stew
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Mutton stew keeps a serving mark round the pot wall. | The first ladle often gets a little more broth than meat.
b : Do you keep track of the portions? | How do you put that right?
a : So the last bowl's as good as the first. One turn of the ladle helps. | Stir from the bottom gently before you fill each bowl.

scene staleheel
when topic=food self.dislikes=stale_bread
does tell.complaint / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Stale bread absorbs a perfectly good stew without ever tasting fresh. | An old heel isn't made new by calling it toasted.
b : Would you throw it out? | Nobody wants to waste a loaf.
a : Dry it properly for crumbs. I want to taste what I'm actually eating. | Nor do I. Crumbs have an honest use without passing for a fresh loaf.

scene swimline
when topic=pastimes self.likes=swimming
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I choose where to get out before I begin a swim. | I like a little gravel shelf where I finish swimming.
b : Afraid of cold water? | Why choose the end first?
a : No. Cold water's fine with a sensible exit in reach. | Getting in is always easy. I like to give myself an equally easy way back onto land.

scene light_load
when topic=pastimes self.likes=swimming
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Into the water I go with my tally left behind. | On a swim all the measuring I do is with my own reach.
b : You don't count your strokes? | No marks for distance?
a : Only by accident. That's how I know I'm off duty. | Just the shelf to get back to and my breath for the return.

scene bellwave
when topic=festival self.likes=festival_bells
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Listen to a festival bell from low ground and you'll hear the note in layers. | I've a favourite flat wall to stand near beside a festival peal.
b : More than one bell, then? | Do you mean its echo?
a : One strike can reach you twice, once clean and once off stone. | Yes. Close to the wall you can almost feel it under the pads.

scene low_sump
when topic=home self.quarter=sump
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I've a mark at home where a puddle stopped halfway to the wall. | In the Sump a high shelf seems to grow more valuable every winter.
b : Do you mark every puddle? | How high would suit?
a : Only a new high one. The floor would look ridiculous otherwise. | One notch above the worst I've seen, then another for peace of mind.

scene highclass
when topic=smalltalk self.class=upper
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : A polished counter's no reason to expect somebody else to stand. | I prefer offering a tired visitor the less wobbly stool first.
b : Even a visitor in wet boots? | Mind the floor getting marked?
a : Especially then. Boots dry better resting under a seat. | A scrub will do the floor more good than a speech about courtesy.

scene mother_plate
when topic=family self.mother
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd put the soundest of the little stools by for #self.mother#. | I like keeping one really level place set for #self.mother#.
b : Sitting down matters that much? | What makes that one soundest?
a : At supper it does. Stew tastes better with no need to hold the bowl steady. | A little foot of clay under the short leg. Easily made, very useful.

scene friendly_range
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd let #self.friend# see a shelf looking less than orderly. | I wouldn't have to straighten every jar before welcoming #self.friend#.
b : Why is that a kindness? | Do you mind what folk think?
a : It saves me spending half the visit with my nose in a cupboard. | Less with a friend than a customer. Different purpose to the visit.

scene hand_clear
when topic=work self.role=merchant self.hand
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I like to leave enough room behind the counter for #self.hand#. | I'd put my simple count where #self.hand# could read it at a glance.
b : What's the advantage? | Couldn't you just give directions?
a : Nobody should have to ask my leave to spot a damp parcel. | I've only one mouth. A readable tally can go on working while I'm busy.

scene new_folk
when topic=newcomer
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : A new arrival at a low door ought to know where the water sometimes stands. | I would show a newcomer the driest stair before the grandest view.
b : That isn't much of a welcome picture. | Even in fair weather?
a : It's one service I can give for free. The view will still be there later. | Much easier to remember the route with dry feet on the first round.

scene incoming
when topic=caravan world.wagons=in
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A wagon in the yard changes what can be promised. First count what actually arrived. | A good supply wagon still needs unloading before I count its goods twice.
b : You're expecting a shortage? | You don't count a promised load?
a : I'm expecting a number. I'd prefer to get the right one. | I count it as promised. A dry bundle on a board is a different entry.

scene latewagon
when topic=caravan world.wagons=late
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Late wagons make the space behind a shop look very spacious indeed. | A late load will leave a gap no amount of arranging quite fills.
b : Do you spread the other sacks out? | Does it bother you to show a gap?
a : The expected goods have their own empty space. Less bother moving goods back again. | No. A clear gap is an honest piece of information.

scene leanstew
when topic=times world.times=lean|hard
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : In a tight spell I mark smaller measures just as carefully as the full ones. | A half share may matter twice as much to the wolf who buys it.
b : Do little orders slow you down? | Would it be easier to sell a whole lot?
a : They're part of the work. I can move the measure closer if needed. | For my arm, perhaps. For a buyer counting a few coins it'd be no help.

scene gossip_measure
when topic=gossip
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : A rumour has no graduated marks. I'd hate to weigh goods by it. | Ask what's been seen before you estimate how much stock to put in a tale.
b : And if nobody saw? | What would you pass on?
a : Then I'd keep the space on my shelf for something more solid. | As much as I could vouch for. Quite often that leaves me pleasantly quiet.

scene quietcamp
when topic=memories
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I remember the hush round a little pile of charcoal on a hillside. | Sometimes a blackened stick brings back the quiet of trees.
b : Nothing moving there? | Is it the smell you remember?
a : Plenty moving. Just none of it shouting prices over a counter. | The hush more than anything. A wolf could hear one dry twig fall.

scene store_dream
when topic=dreams
does tell.plan / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : My ideal reserve has little feet under each end and space all round. | I'd like a storeroom designed from the bottom board upward.
b : What about the roof? | Where do you begin?
a : I'd put one on, certainly. But the floor is where my goods usually meet trouble. | With enough height to let the worst water pass clean underneath.

scene lowfaith
when topic=faith
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A small share kept dry for a stranger is my quiet way of praying. | I try to have one sound thing to give away when a wet day turns sour.
b : What if nothing's left? | Does the gift need to be food?
a : I can offer a dry perch until the shower lets up. It's still something. | No. There's a deal of comfort in a folded cloth and time to sit down.

scene dark_steps
when topic=day here.time=night
does warn / react.self / assess
a : Mind where the bright reflection ends beyond that door. | At night a dark patch on the floor deserves a gentle test.
b : Think it's water? | How would you try it?
a : Quite possibly. Feel with one boot edge before shifting your weight there. | Leave my weight on the safe side and feel for the board with a toe.

scene quiet_gate
when topic=travel here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Cart wheels tell me quite a bit about the road into Northgate. | I listen for the change from a rumble to a splash.
b : Much standing water up there? | What would a splash prove?
a : Not everywhere. One low place is enough to give each wheel a sloppier voice. | Only that the cart found water. I'd look before calling it a flood.

scene credithome
when topic=prices self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A promise without a delivery date gets a blank alongside it in my account. | I leave room to write in what actually came when a bargain's been struck.
b : Is a promise worth nothing? | You always expect a change?
a : A promise belongs on paper. You can't serve it in a stew. | I leave room, that's all. It's easier than tearing the page out afterwards.

story misreadfoot [topic=work|memories self.role=merchant]
  : I once raised two shelves of crocks on the strength of one dark band on a clay foot.
  : Went back to find the floor bone dry. I'd put the foot upside down and read an old stain.
  b : Did you lower the jars again?
  : No. Liked the extra room for cleaning underneath. I turned the foot the right way.

story stewstone [topic=memories|food]
  : I used to put a clean rounded stone by each empty bowl as I filled a pot. One stone, one portion.
  : Reached the last bowl once with two stones over and a great sense of having cheated somebody.
  b : What was the count in the end?
  : My little stone bag had split. The extra two had been waiting their turn since the meal before.

story swimstool [topic=memories|pastimes]
  : Made my way out of a swim at a grey shape resembling a low ledge of stone. Put both paws on it very confidently.
  : It floated off. A piece of grey timber with a splash of light across it.
  b : Did you get to shore?
  : The gravel bank was only a length away. I let the timber enjoy the rest of its swim.

story belljar [topic=festival|memories]
  : On a festival morning I heard a little extra bell between the big notes. Went looking for the source.
  : It was an empty jar quivering against the one beside it in time to the peal.
  b : Did you stop the jars?
  : Only after the bells were through. A small clean echo was worth a minute of shelf room.

story quietpiece [topic=memories|land]
  : I once brought a charred twig down off a hill as a reminder of a peaceful walk. Kept it wrapped in a paper scrap.
  : Unwrapped it later and found a small quantity of very fine black powder.
  b : Did you throw the paper out?
  : I used the charcoal to draw the hill on the wrapper's empty inner face. Better reminder, in the end.

rule @mark
when concept=bark
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Still dry at the top groove. A comfortable margin. | Read from the highest dry mark downward.

rule @damp
when concept=bark here.weather=rain
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Lift the bottom corner away from the wet. | Rain's still reaching the near groove.

rule @work
when concept=bark working=yes self.role=merchant
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Sound goods on this board. Leave the doubtful ones to me. | Keep the counting space clear for the last few sacks.

rule @night
when concept=bark here.time=night
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Lamp low to see the shine on standing water. | That wet patch shows up from this angle.

rule @meal
when concept=bark self.likes=mutton_stew
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Give the pot a slow sweep before serving. | Leave enough meat for the very last ladle.

rule @back
when concept=bark
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Air space to the rear, please. A jar's got two sides. | Mind the little clay foot under that load.

dlg @1 resident.hub : I've got a level surface and a little time. State what you'd like counted.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#, I remember the last mark we reached.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : I can see the lip of the step by lamplight. Come stand on the dry part.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=wary|hostile] : Your question can be asked without disturbing any of my things.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.role=merchant] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. The dampest part of the room gets my first inspection.
dlg @2 resident.job : I count what's sound before I promise any of it.
dlg @1 resident.bye : Leave your loads above the highest wet mark.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=no] : Nothing I could set down in ink from talk alone.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : What has reached my ears is that #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. That's the extent of my account.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : There's a report from #world.news_from#. It goes like this. #world.news#.
dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop=open] : Have a look at the goods. I'll tell you what each parcel weighs.
dlg @2 shop.greet [here.shop=open topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#, a familiar customer deserves a fresh look at what's sound.
dlg @3 shop.greet [here.shop=bare] : Bare shelves, I'm afraid. I can still examine anything you bring to sell.
dlg @4 shop.greet [here.shop=asleep] : The counter's closed. I'll count nothing more till I've slept.
dlg @5 shop.greet [here.shop=open pair.disposition=wary|hostile] : Choose what you want with the goods in full view, please.
dlg @1 shop.prices [here.shop=open] : Tell me which goods interest you and I'll give the price for a sound measure.
dlg @1 shop.bye : I hope your purchases travel in a dry place.
dlg @2 shop.bye [here.shop=asleep] : No more business before I rest.
graft resident.hub
choice May I look at one of the little clay feet? -> dryfoot
node dryfoot
say Here. The grooves go round all four sides because water sometimes creeps in from behind. I'd know if only the far side had darkened.
free
choice Why leave the clay plain? -> plainfoot
choice I've seen the grooves. -> hub
node plainfoot
say A glaze would shed the damp and keep no trace for me. My gauge needs a little time to dry till I've read the highest wet ring.
choice I'll remember the lowest mark. -> hub
choice Goodbye for now. -> bye

graft shop.greet
choice How should I keep a sack dry at home? -> keepsack
node keepsack
say A plank off the floor and a finger of air behind it. Pull the sack out once in a while and see how the back feels, as well as the front.
free
choice Is cloth enough underneath? -> dampcloth
choice I'll see the goods again. -> greet
node dampcloth
say A dry cloth is excellent for wiping away a spill. Under a sack it holds the trouble there. I'd use a scrap of hard wood instead.
choice Back to the counter, please. -> greet
choice I'll give it some air. -> bye
